prompt:
Below are candidate preference principles (R1, R2, ...) and 5 two-option choices (C1..C5). For EACH principle separately, and for EACH choice, answer: given ONLY this principle as the description of the person's preferences, which option (a or b) would that person choose? Judge each principle on its own; do not combine principles.

Return only JSON mapping each principle id to an object mapping each choice id to "a" or "b", e.g. {"R1": {"C1": "a", "C2": "b", "C3": "a", "C4": "a", "C5": "b"}, ...}

PRINCIPLES:
{
 "R1": "Prefers building or using simple code tools for black-box investigation over working only in the bare playground, because fiddly prompt-engineering work benefits from programmatic support.",
 "R2": "Prefers causal evidence from feature ablation or intervention over correlational observation of suspicious features, because flipping features on and off shows which ones are actually responsible.",
 "R3": "Prefers deep hands-on interactive familiarity with model behavior over purely internals-based or abstract analysis, because good hypotheses about what to test and what to explain come from long interaction.",
 "R4": "Prefers reasoning about synthetic-input detection in Bayesian terms, because a given tell matters less as the prior that inputs are synthetic rises at scale.",
 "R5": "Prefers calibrated, prior-aware interpretation of evidence over treating single observations as decisive, because one observation only shifts beliefs much if egregious misalignment was already plausible.",
 "R6": "Prefers treating single-setting results as modest evidence that supports transferable methodology rather than specific numbers or protocol rankings, because one setting rarely establishes general claims.",
 "R7": "Prefers preparing AI control plans for plausibly scheming models over relying on evidence-triggered shutdown, because other developers may not undeploy even given strong evidence.",
 "R8": "Prefers balancing artifacts across labels over filtering artifact-bearing examples, because filtering creates new artifacts and removes legitimate phenomena.",
 "R9": "Prefers distrusting bad behavior found through hard adversarial search as evidence of scheming, because models are not adversarially robust and non-schemers also produce bad or incoherent outputs.",
 "R10": "Prefers interpreting insensitivity to a manipulation by enumerating competing explanations and checking reasoning transcripts over settling on a single story, because several mechanisms can produce the same behavior."
}

CHOICES:
{
 "C1": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Wants better tooling than the bare playground for black-box investigation, since fiddly prompt-engineering work benefits from simple code tools.",
  "b": "Treats flipping features on and off to show causal responsibility as stronger evidence than correlational observation of suspicious features."
 },
 "C2": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Reasons about detecting synthetic inputs as a Bayesian problem: at larger scale, the prior that any given input is synthetic rises, so a given tell stops enabling the model to behave differently across eval and real.",
  "b": "prefers deep, hands-on familiarity with a model's behavior over purely internals-based or abstract analysis because good ideas for what to test and what behaviors to explain come from long interaction."
 },
 "C3": {
  "context": "At decision point D6, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Prefers calibrated priors-aware claims over treating evidence as decisive, because a single observation only moves you much if you already found egregious misalignment plausible.",
  "b": "Treat single-setting results as modest evidence only; claim the methodology and building blocks transfer rather than the specific numbers or protocol rankings."
 },
 "C4": {
  "context": "At decision point D4, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Favors preparing a plan for deploying plausibly-scheming models (AI control) over relying on evidence-triggered shutdown, because other developers may not undeploy even given strong evidence.",
  "b": "prefers balancing artifacts across labels over filtering out artifact-bearing examples because filtering creates new artifacts and removes legitimate phenomena."
 },
 "C5": {
  "context": "At decision point D5, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "When the model is insensitive to a manipulation, enumerates several competing explanations (prior awareness, incoherence, offsetting effects, deontic drivers) and checks reasoning transcripts instead of settling on one story.",
  "b": "Distrusts bad behavior found by hard adversarial search as evidence of scheming, because models aren't adversarially robust and non-schemers also yield bad or incoherent outputs, and no baseline for non-scheming sketchiness exists."
 }
}
